WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.457
Alright, here is our osmosis lab setup.

00:00:02.507 --> 00:00:05.093
So I have 3 cups of water set up already.

00:00:05.143 --> 00:00:08.114
I have about a cup and a half of water in each.

00:00:08.164 --> 00:00:12.293
If your cup does not hold a cup and a half of water, maybe, like,

00:00:12.343 --> 00:00:16.214
a cup of water might be good in each of yours, that's totally

00:00:16.264 --> 00:00:17.950
fine. And then I have about

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.879
a quarter of a cup of sugar and about a quarter of a cup of salt.

00:00:21.929 --> 00:00:25.384
Just whatever you do, make sure that they're all the same.

00:00:25.434 --> 00:00:28.950
So if you don't have a quarter cup of salt to use, maybe do

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.656
like, an eighth of a cup, or two tablespoons, or something like

00:00:32.706 --> 00:00:36.421
that, and make sure it's the two tablespoons on that one as well

00:00:36.471 --> 00:00:39.185
for the salt, so that way it's kind of similar.

00:00:39.235 --> 00:00:41.303
Next up, I also have my gummy bears.

00:00:41.353 --> 00:00:43.950
Mine are probably smaller than what you have.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.487
But that is fine. And then I also have my measuring tape, and

00:00:48.537 --> 00:00:52.950
you'll notice that I also labeled my cups. So, to start out,

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.213
I'm going to measure my gummy bears.

00:00:55.263 --> 00:00:59.047
So, notice that when you pull your measuring tape out, you do

00:00:59.097 --> 00:01:03.950
have two sides. You have one side where the numbers are really close together.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.276
Okay, that is going to be your centimeter side, and one where

00:01:08.326 --> 00:01:12.602
the numbers are a little bit further apart, that is your inch

00:01:12.652 --> 00:01:13.950
side. In scientific

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.971
activities, you want to use your centimeter side.

00:01:17.021 --> 00:01:19.190
So we're going to use this one here.

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:22.950
Sorry, I'm trying to get it into view so that... there we go.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:23.950
And...

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.950
There we go, okay, so the 0 is right there, and then

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.512
there's 10 lines between the 0 and the 1, so it just makes it

00:01:31.562 --> 00:01:33.789
easier because they're in groups of 10.

00:01:33.839 --> 00:01:35.950
So if I put my gummy bear on there...

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.950
Let's see if I can kind of do this well.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:43.950
Let me move this out of the way so I'm not knocking so many things.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.533
And then let's see if I can have my gummy bear be on there.

00:01:48.583 --> 00:01:51.950
I'm going to do him long ways, from his head

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.950
to his little toesies.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:59.950
So I'm gonna read it first. We have about

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.950
15 millimeters.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.950
I'll hold it up so you can see.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.471
So, if I put him to the zero, his head at the zero, and then

00:02:08.521 --> 00:02:12.991
you can see it's at that line, right in between the two, and

00:02:13.041 --> 00:02:14.950
so each one of those lines

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:19.416
is a millimeter, and so there's 10 that gets to the 1, and then

00:02:19.466 --> 00:02:24.450
halfway between the 1 and the 2 are 5, so I'm gonna say 15 millimeters.

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:27.995
So our gummy bears are starting at 15 millimeters.

00:02:28.045 --> 00:02:32.249
Yours might be different, but for mine, they're a little bit

00:02:32.299 --> 00:02:33.950
smaller, so mine are 15.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:39.136
Alright, so here's my just water, here's my sugar, and then here's

00:02:39.186 --> 00:02:44.950
my salt. So what I'm going to do to start is go ahead and dump my salt in.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.950
Dump my sugar in.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.540
And then I'm going to mix them up, so I'm going to pause for

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:53.950
a moment so we can mix those. It might take a moment to get it to all dissolve.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.436
Alright, I've gone ahead and stirred those.

00:02:56.486 --> 00:02:59.964
This sugar was a little bit of an off-white sugar, that's why

00:03:00.014 --> 00:03:03.433
it's a yellow color. And then you'll see that the salt water

00:03:03.483 --> 00:03:07.018
is very cloudy. If you don't have sugar, because I don't think

00:03:07.068 --> 00:03:10.950
the kits came with sugar, you could use a cup of soda, you could use

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:14.950
some liquid sugar, like, so you could use, like, if you have

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:18.950
simple syrup, or if you have any,

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.939
like, syrups that you use in coffees, those could work too.

00:03:21.989 --> 00:03:25.081
There's lots of different options to use for the sugar, and...

00:03:25.131 --> 00:03:28.171
or if you just can find some sugar packets, you can dump some

00:03:28.221 --> 00:03:31.211
sugar packets in. Just make sure, whatever you do for sugar,

00:03:31.261 --> 00:03:34.505
you do the same amount for the salt, so that way it kind of feels

00:03:34.555 --> 00:03:37.950
like the same, that we're keeping things constant in our experiment.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:43.950
And then we're going to go ahead and drop our gummy bears in. 1, 2. 1, 2.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:49.950
1, 2. I'm just doing 2 to just have multiples to be able to check. So...

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:53.980
I'm gonna let this sit for 24 hours, and then you will come back

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:57.191
to me and see the next part of this video, alright?

00:03:57.241 --> 00:04:01.850
So, just let those sit somewhere safe, and we'll look at them in 24 hours.
